1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,350
Mrs.

2
00:00:01,011 --> 00:00:05,487
Rao pays one hundred and twenty
rupees for a Laddoo Lab 6-pack.

3
00:00:05,787 --> 00:00:09,721
So Arjun calls her a one hundred
and twenty rupee customer.

4
00:00:10,271 --> 00:00:11,398
Zoya disagrees.

5
00:00:11,398 --> 00:00:12,298
Mrs.

6
00:00:11,911 --> 00:00:14,958
Rao buys every school
week, year after year.

7
00:00:14,958 --> 00:00:16,979
So what is she really worth?

8
00:00:17,629 --> 00:00:19,352
Every school morning, Mrs.

9
00:00:19,352 --> 00:00:22,069
Rao packs a laddoo into
her daughter's tiffin.

10
00:00:22,419 --> 00:00:26,916
Now Dev wants to spend five hundred
rupees on free trial packs, to win

11
00:00:26,916 --> 00:00:28,719
each new parent like her.

12
00:00:29,069 --> 00:00:32,478
Arjun asks: is one customer
really worth that much?

13
00:00:33,128 --> 00:00:37,456
To answer, marketers use
customer lifetime value, or CLV.

14
00:00:37,756 --> 00:00:41,720
It is the total profit a customer
brings you, over the whole time they

15
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:42,718
stay with you.

16
00:00:43,068 --> 00:00:44,166
Not one sale.

17
00:00:44,166 --> 00:00:45,886
Every sale, for years.

18
00:00:46,236 --> 00:00:50,723
The simple formula: profit per year,
times the years they stay, minus what

19
00:00:50,723 --> 00:00:52,163
it cost to win them.

20
00:00:52,813 --> 00:00:54,638
Let's work it out for Mrs.

21
00:00:54,638 --> 00:00:56,492
Rao, with example figures.

22
00:00:56,842 --> 00:00:57,742
Step one.

23
00:00:57,654 --> 00:00:58,873
Profit per pack.

24
00:00:58,873 --> 00:01:03,420
A pack sells for one hundred and
twenty rupees, and costs eighty to make

25
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:04,380
and deliver.

26
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:06,570
So each pack earns forty rupees.

27
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:07,820
Step two.

28
00:01:07,746 --> 00:01:08,985
Profit per year.

29
00:01:08,985 --> 00:01:13,186
She buys one pack every school
week, about forty weeks a year.

30
00:01:13,536 --> 00:01:18,300
Forty packs times forty rupees is one
thousand six hundred rupees a year.

31
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:19,550
Step three.

32
00:01:19,539 --> 00:01:20,728
Years she stays.

33
00:01:20,728 --> 00:01:23,811
Her daughter has five
more years at this school.

34
00:01:24,161 --> 00:01:28,859
One thousand six hundred, times five,
is eight thousand rupees of profit.

35
00:01:29,209 --> 00:01:30,109
Step four.

36
00:01:30,065 --> 00:01:31,326
Use today's money.

37
00:01:31,326 --> 00:01:35,295
A rupee next year is worth a
little less than a rupee today.

38
00:01:35,645 --> 00:01:39,865
At ten percent a year, those eight
thousand rupees are worth about six

39
00:01:39,865 --> 00:01:41,818
thousand and sixty five today.

40
00:01:42,168 --> 00:01:45,566
Take away the five hundred
it cost to win her.

41
00:01:45,566 --> 00:01:46,466
Mrs.

42
00:01:46,116 --> 00:01:50,830
Rao is worth about five thousand
five hundred and sixty five rupees.

43
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:52,840
Don't count the sale.

44
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:54,098
Count the customer.

45
00:01:54,798 --> 00:01:57,254
Every part of the formula is a lever.

46
00:01:57,254 --> 00:01:59,843
Pull any one, and lifetime value grows.

47
00:02:00,193 --> 00:02:01,725
More profit per sale.

48
00:02:01,725 --> 00:02:05,565
A phone maker sells a cover
and earphones with every phone.

49
00:02:05,915 --> 00:02:07,022
Buy more often.

50
00:02:07,022 --> 00:02:10,749
A coffee shop gives a stamp
card, so you come back sooner.

51
00:02:11,099 --> 00:02:12,097
Stay longer.

52
00:02:12,097 --> 00:02:16,338
A gym offers a cheaper yearly
plan, so members stay all year.

53
00:02:16,688 --> 00:02:17,942
Cost less to win.

54
00:02:17,942 --> 00:02:20,880
A bank rewards customers
who bring in a friend.

55
00:02:21,530 --> 00:02:23,751
But should every customer get a number?

56
00:02:23,751 --> 00:02:25,370
It depends how you use it.

57
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:26,781
The good side.

58
00:02:26,781 --> 00:02:30,977
You spend wisely, and you look after
customers who matter over time.

59
00:02:31,327 --> 00:02:32,527
The hard side.

60
00:02:32,527 --> 00:02:37,562
CLV is only a guess about the future,
and small buyers can get ignored.

61
00:02:37,912 --> 00:02:40,450
In 2002, Werner Reinartz and V.

62
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:43,369
Kumar studied sixteen thousand customers.

63
00:02:43,369 --> 00:02:47,108
Loyal customers were not
always the most profitable.

64
00:02:47,458 --> 00:02:52,166
So Arjun wants to stop serving a
small shop that buys one pack a month.

65
00:02:52,516 --> 00:02:53,563
Nani says no.

66
00:02:53,563 --> 00:02:56,247
Every customer gets the same fresh laddoo.

67
00:02:56,247 --> 00:03:00,541
So Zoya keeps the shop, and spends
the offers where they pay back.

68
00:03:00,891 --> 00:03:01,896
The lesson?

69
00:03:01,896 --> 00:03:06,004
Use CLV to decide where to
invest, not whom to respect.

70
00:03:06,654 --> 00:03:07,624
Quick check.

71
00:03:07,624 --> 00:03:08,524
Mrs.

72
00:03:08,134 --> 00:03:12,565
Rao brings one thousand six hundred
rupees of profit a year, and stays

73
00:03:12,565 --> 00:03:13,485
five years.

74
00:03:13,485 --> 00:03:15,845
What is her simple lifetime profit?

75
00:03:17,745 --> 00:03:19,199
Eight thousand rupees!

76
00:03:19,199 --> 00:03:22,420
One thousand six hundred
a year, times five years.

77
00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:24,030
So, remember.

78
00:03:24,329 --> 00:03:29,507
CLV is the total profit a customer
brings, over all the years they stay.

79
00:03:29,857 --> 00:03:34,549
Profit per year, times years, minus
the cost to win them, in today's money.

80
00:03:34,899 --> 00:03:39,533
Raise it with more profit per sale,
more visits, longer stays, and cheaper

81
00:03:39,533 --> 00:03:40,433
wins.

82
00:03:40,670 --> 00:03:45,456
Next time, in our final episode, we'll
build Laddoo Lab's yearly scorecard:

83
00:03:45,456 --> 00:03:47,413
marketing performance metrics.
